    {
      "type": "prose",
      "caption": "Summary",
      "html": "<p>The aorist passive participle is made on the aorist passive stem without augment: λυθείς, λυθεῖσα, λυθέν, having been loosed (γραφείς, having been written, from the second aorist passive). A participle with a noun or pronoun in the genitive, standing outside the structure of the rest of the sentence, is a genitive absolute: πορευθέντος τοῦ ἄρχοντος οἱ δοῦλοι εἶπον ταῦτα, when the ruler had gone, the servants said these things. It is used only when the noun is not the subject or object of the main verb.</p>"
    },
